{
 "node": "SpatialSound",
 "mode": "scene",
 "note": "the node's gain stage (intensity 0..1 times gain, stereo explicit) and the Web Audio panner (distance model and rolloff, with LINEAR held to rolloff 1, reference and maximum distance, cone angles from radians to degrees held to 0..360, cone outer gain, HRTF or equal power) are compared with X_ITE 16.6.0, and so is the panner's position and orientation: location and direction taken through the Transform and the camera (a viewpoint at 0 0 10) into view space. Runs in the real Babylon body. The panner gets a stereo input (a mono source is up-mixed to two equal channels, in both engines), so the stereo panning of the Web Audio specification applies, not the mono one: a source far to the left is twice as loud in the left ear as the mono formula says (0.2747 against 0.1373 at 5.1 distance). Signal compared with X_ITE 16.6.0 and with the Web Audio specification's panner formulas on the page's real Web Audio clock: a 1 kHz sine (rms 0.7071) from a SpatialSound is recorded as two channels, left and right, and each channel's rms is held to the expected distance gain times the equal power pan gain (azimuth from the view space position, camera at 0 0 10) within 0.012: a source on the left (view position -5 0 -1: left 0.137, right 0.014), on the right, straight ahead at distance 4 (INVERSE: gain 0.25 split 0.707 / 0.707), LINEAR with maxDistance 10, EXPONENTIAL with rolloff 2, intensity 0.5 and gain 0.5 (a quarter), a cone (direction away from the listener, outer angle 1 rad, outer gain 0.2: a fifth), the same cone facing the listener (no loss) and referenceDistance 4; with enableHRTF (a stereo input is panned by the HRTF as two channels, so the ratio is about 2.2, not that of the equal power model) the left ear is louder than the right (rms 0.0288 against 0.0132) and equals X_ITE's; enabled = false is silent. Ours and X_ITE agree within 0.012 on every case.",
